[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the delivery question, and there's a constraint here I want to be explicit about because it's the main thing that
  disappoints people about client-side notifications.

  [1mA page with no server cannot reach you when it isn't open.[0m Real background notifications — the kind that buzz your
  phone at 9am about a task due today — require a service worker plus a push service plus a server holding subscriptions
  and VAPID keys. That's re-introducing the entire backend we just decided not to build. So the honest options:

  - [1mIn-app attention list.[0m When you open the app, overdue and stale tasks are surfaced at the top, visually distinct,
    with a count. No permissions, no prompts, works everywhere, impossible to break. The catch: it only tells you when
    you're already looking.
  - [1mBrowser notifications while the tab is open.[0m OS-level toasts via the Notification API. Needs a permission prompt,
    and still only fires while the page is running — so in practice it tells you about a task becoming overdue only if
    you happened to leave the tab open across the deadline. Modest addition on top of the attention list.
  - [1mReal push.[0m Service worker + push server + backend. Genuinely notifies you when the app is closed, and reverses the
    single-user/no-server decision.

  [1mMy recommendation: the in-app attention list, and treat browser notifications as a maybe-later.[0m For a personal task
  list the realistic usage is that you open it when you sit down to work — which is exactly when a prominent "3 things
  need attention" is useful and a toast three hours earlier would have been forgotten. It's also the only option that's
  fully reliable. If it turns out you genuinely need to be interrupted when the app is closed, that's a real requirement
  and worth a server, but I'd want you to discover that from use rather than assume it now.
